---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';

const pubs = (await getCollection('publications')).sort(
  (a, b) => (a.data.order ?? 999) - (b.data.order ?? 999),
);
const publications = await Promise.all(
  pubs.map(async (p) => ({ ...p, Content: (await render(p)).Content })),
);
---

<BaseLayout title="Thesis & Publications">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Ph.D. Thesis</h1>
    <p class="lead">Machine Learning Techniques for Electricity Price Forecasting</p>

    <p class="pub-links">
      <a class="button" href="/files/thesis.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">↓ Manuscript (PDF)</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/files/defence.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">↓ Defence slides</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="https://www.youtube.com/live/Stj9HCPnH7g?si=Ta0hoN0RIBp3b4ER&t=226" target="_blank" rel="noopener">▶ Defence video</a>
    </p>

    <figure>
      <img src="/files/fee.jpg" alt="La fée électricité, a large painting depicting the many uses of electricity" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>
        <i>La fée électricité</i>, by Raoul Dufy (1937), gives a good overview of the
        complexity of the problem.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Background</h2>
    <p>
      I started this thesis following my research internship at{' '}
      <a href="https://elmy.fr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ELMY</a>{' '}
      (<a href="/experience/">more on that here</a>). The thesis was a collaboration between
      the{' '}
      <a href="https://liris.cnrs.fr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LIRIS laboratory</a>{' '}
      (INSA Lyon), part of the{' '}
      <a href="https://www.cnrs.fr/en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNRS</a>, and ELMY. As part
      of the{' '}
      <a href="https://projet.liris.cnrs.fr/dm2l/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DM2L research team</a>,
      I was mentored by{' '}
      <a href="https://perso.liris.cnrs.fr/celine.robardet/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Céline Robardet</a>{' '}
      and <a href="https://www.lrde.epita.fr/wiki/User:Marc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marc Plantevit</a>.
      I defended on 17 January 2024 and was granted the title of Doctor in Computer Science.
    </p>

    <h2>Abstract</h2>
    <p>
      Electricity is essential for the energy transition due to the diversity of greenhouse-gas-free
      means of production and its potential to replace fossil fuels in transportation, heating and
      industry. However, it requires a constant balance between generation and consumption to
      maintain intensity in the network, and it cannot be stored efficiently. It is then necessary to
      use a Price Fixing Algorithm (PFA) for developing competitive markets. Daily, the European PFA
      EUPHEMIA determines the prices for the next day in Europe — the Day-Ahead prices — that maximize
      social welfare while maintaining energy balance. Unlike other purely speculative markets, the
      Day-Ahead prices are algorithmically computed. Forecasting them is thus a unique and challenging
      task.
    </p>
    <p>
      This introduces the problem of Electricity Price Forecasting (EPF) at the European scale, which
      consists in predicting the 24 hourly prices for each market before their fixation at 12am. The
      literature highlights two approaches: Expert Models, that aim at replicating the PFA and
      computing the prices based on estimates of EUPHEMIA's inputs, and Data-Driven methods that
      directly estimate prices using exogenous variables and past prices. Both approaches are
      incomplete: Expert Models are theoretically appealing but fail to produce accurate forecasts in
      practice; conversely, Data-Driven approaches lack transparency, lowering forecast reliability.
      Moreover, the true relationship between variables and prices is only captured by EUPHEMIA,
      implicitly limiting the performance of Data-Driven approaches.
    </p>
    <p>
      This thesis addresses those limitations. The first challenge is to produce accurate and
      explainable models for a given market. We achieve the former by extending methodologies from the
      literature, while we use Shap values — a model-agnostic explainability tool — for the latter.
      Then, we build a multi-market forecasting model by representing the European network as a graph
      where each market is a node labeled with its prices; graph edges are connection lines between
      markets, and we estimate cross-market flows using an optimization problem prior to training.
      Lastly, we combine the EUPHEMIA algorithm with a neural network that forecasts its inputs. To
      account for the price-forecasting error during training, we compute the gradient of EUPHEMIA's
      output with respect to its input by vanishing the derivative of the dual function using a
      dichotomy search.
    </p>
    <p>
      I believe this thesis will benefit EPF practitioners and contribute toward bridging the gap
      between Expert Models and Data-Driven approaches. I also believe our work on mixing optimization
      problems with machine-learning models will benefit the broader scientific community.
    </p>

    <h2 id="publications">Publications</h2>
    <p>Three publications in international journals and conferences came out of this thesis.</p>
    <ol class="entries">
      {
        publications.map(({ data, Content }) => (
          <li class="entry">
            <div class="entry-head">
              <h3>{data.title}</h3>
              {data.award && <span class="badge">🏆 {data.award}</span>}
            </div>
            <p class="org">{data.venue} · {data.authors}</p>
            <p class="pub-links">
              {data.paperUrl && (
                <a href={data.paperUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paper (PDF)</a>
              )}
              {data.slidesUrl && (
                <a href={data.slidesUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Slides</a>
              )}
              {data.doi && (
                <a href={data.doi} target="_blank" rel="noopener">DOI</a>
              )}
            </p>
            <details class="abstract">
              <summary>Abstract</summary>
              <Content />
            </details>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
